INT. GREENHOUSE ON A DISUSED FERRY - EVENING

A car ferry long retired from its route, moored and rusting at a quiet estuary. Its vehicle deck has been roofed with glass and turned into a nursery of citrus trees in tubs. Lamps hum. The air is warm, bitter-sweet, and smells of leaf and engine oil.

ISOLDE FAIRWEATHER-NKEMELU, 34, sleeves rolled, soil to the elbows, prunes a lemon tree with more force than the tree needs. Her wedding ring hangs on a string around her neck instead of her finger.

Across the aisle, DESMOND ACHTERBERG, 36, in a good coat that does not belong among the tubs, holds a paper bag of pastries as if it were a summons. He has not put it down since he arrived.

Between them, perched on an upturned crate and reading a seed catalogue she is not reading, sits MARGUERITE "GRETA" OYELARAN, 70, Isolde's landlady and the ferry's last pilot.

Isolde snips a branch. It falls. Desmond watches it fall.

DESMOND
You've moved the tangerines.

ISOLDE
They wanted light.

DESMOND
They used to be by the stairs.

ISOLDE
Things shift.

GRETA
(turning a page)
The lemons shifted too. Nobody asked them.

Desmond sets the bag down on a workbench. Takes it up again.

DESMOND
I brought the ones with the almond.

ISOLDE
I don't eat those anymore.

DESMOND
Since when?

ISOLDE
Since a while.

DESMOND
You loved those.

ISOLDE
I loved a lot of things at the time.

GRETA
There's a draught from the east vent. Someone should mind it.

Neither of them moves toward the vent.

DESMOND
The bank wrote to me about the account. They said you'd closed your half.

ISOLDE
I did.

DESMOND
You could have called.

ISOLDE
I could have.

DESMOND
I'd have said yes.

ISOLDE
You'd have said yes to anything, that month.

DESMOND
That's not fair.

ISOLDE
It isn't a verdict. It's a date.

GRETA
Margins are thin in citrus. People forget that.

DESMOND
Greta, would you mind--

GRETA
I would. I've a view of the water from here and I'm using it.

Isolde almost smiles. Catches herself. Snips.

DESMOND
Your mother sent a card. To the flat. I didn't know what to do with it.

ISOLDE
Put it in the drawer.

DESMOND
I did. I took it out again.

ISOLDE
And?

DESMOND
It's in my coat.

He does not reach for it. His hand rests on the pocket.

ISOLDE
Keep it.

DESMOND
It's addressed to you.

ISOLDE
Then it's mine to say who keeps it.

Silence. A pump clicks on somewhere below decks. Water moves in the pipes.

DESMOND
You've lost weight.

ISOLDE
You've gained a coat.

DESMOND
It was a gift.

ISOLDE
From?

DESMOND
Does it matter?

ISOLDE
Not at all.

She drops the shears into a tub. They ring against the rim. Both of them look at the sound.

GRETA
(to the catalogue)
Eighty-one pounds for a Buddha's hand citron. Robbery.

DESMOND
I'm not here about the account.

ISOLDE
I know.

DESMOND
I'm not here about the card.

ISOLDE
I know that too.

DESMOND
The lease on the flat is up in March. I wondered if you had a view.

ISOLDE
On the flat.

DESMOND
On the flat.

ISOLDE
(a long beat)
The east vent really is open.

She walks to it. Closes it. Stays there with her back to him, hand flat on the cold iron.

They speak at once.

DESMOND
I don't know how to be in a room with you.

ISOLDE ^
I don't know how to be in a room without you.

Nobody moves. Greta turns a page, slowly, and does not read it.

GRETA
Well. That's the first honest weather of the evening.

Desmond puts the pastry bag down for the last time. Walks the length of the aisle, stops one tub short of Isolde. A lemon hangs between them, green, not yet ready.

He lifts it gently on his palm, as if weighing it. Lets it go.

ISOLDE
It needs another month.

DESMOND
Does it.

ISOLDE
The ones in the light, perhaps three weeks.

She turns. His coat is wet at the shoulder where the roof drips. She notices. Says nothing. Takes the string from around her neck, winds the ring once about her finger, and unwinds it again.

DESMOND
Three weeks.

ISOLDE
The flat can wait three weeks.

GRETA
(standing, folding the catalogue)
I'll put the kettle on. Down in the wheelhouse. It will take me a good long while.

She goes. Her footsteps ring on the iron stairs, slow, deliberate, deliberately loud.

Desmond picks the almond pastry from the bag. Offers it.

Isolde looks at it. Looks at him. Takes half.
